<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest. Required since May 2024 for any app that touches one of
  Apple's "required reason" APIs, and checked at upload rather than at review.

  This one is nearly empty, and that is the honest answer rather than an
  optimistic one. The app has no accounts, no analytics and no third-party SDKs:
  hoard-scan/Package.swift declares no `.package(url:)` at all, every target
  depends only on its siblings and Apple frameworks, and there is no
  Package.resolved because there is nothing to resolve. Reads run on-device
  through CardKit and go to a Mac on the same Wi-Fi. Nothing is sent anywhere
  else, so there is nothing to declare as collected and nobody to name as a
  tracking domain.

  Coverage is a property of the whole linked binary, not of the app target's own
  sources. The grep that produced the one entry below ran across
  scan/hoard-scan-ios/Sources and all four linked package targets (CardKit,
  BorderKit, ScanLink, ScanWire), for each of the four required-reason
  categories:

    - File timestamps: nothing. SessionLog is the only thing here that touches
      the filesystem and it uses FileHandle(forWritingTo:), seekToEnd() and
      write(contentsOf:) — none of which are on the list. It never asks a file
      how old it is, so `creationDate`, `modificationDate`, `getattrlist`,
      `fstat` and `stat` all return no hits.
    - System boot time: nothing. No `systemUptime`, no `mach_absolute_time`.
      The capture loop times itself with `Date`/`CFAbsoluteTime`, which are not
      required-reason APIs.
    - Disk space: nothing. Nothing here asks how full the phone is.
    - Active keyboards: nothing. No `activeInputModes`.

  Re-run that grep after any new dependency lands. A manifest that was correct
  when it was written is not evidence that it is correct now.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  UserDefaults, and it genuinely applies. Two independent readers:
			  DevMode.key through @AppStorage in PairingView.swift and
			  SessionView.swift (@AppStorage *is* UserDefaults, which is easy to
			  forget because it does not look like it), and TierSettings.swift,
			  which holds a UserDefaults directly and reads and writes the
			  "tiers.*" keys for the price tiers and their voices.

			  An earlier note claimed this category did not apply because the
			  pairing code lives in the Keychain. That is true of the code —
			  PairingStore.swift uses SecItem* deliberately, and the Keychain is
			  not a required-reason API — and irrelevant to the preferences.

			  CA92.1 is "access info from the app's own container, or from the
			  app group's container," which is exactly what both readers do:
			  standard defaults, no suite, no app group, nothing read back out.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
